          "date": "2026-10-15 13:13:35.000000000",
          "tz": 120
        },
        "subject": "LU-17180 osc: run writeback work on an unbound workqueue",
        "message": "LU-17180 osc: run writeback work on an unbound workqueue\n\nPatch \"LU-17180 ptlrpc: don't block ptlrpcd too long\" moved the\nosc writeback and LRU work out of ptlrpcd threads, to\nschedule_work(). schedule_work() queues to system_wq, which is\nper-CPU: the work runs on the CPU that queued it. Most of it is\nqueued by the writing process itself, from osc_extent_release(),\nosc_lru_reserve() and osc_lru_del(), so building RPCs and shrinking\nthe LRU now take CPU time from the writer, which is the bottleneck\nof a single-stream buffered write. ptlrpcd threads are not bound\nto one CPU, so before that patch this work ran beside the writer.\n\nWith dd pinned to CPU 3, writing 8 GiB to a 16-stripe file:\n  brw_queue_work: 210ms of its 261ms ran on CPU 3\n  lru_queue_work: 299ms of its 299ms ran on CPU 3\nA drop from 1363 MiB/s to 1198 MiB/s was seen on real hardware.\n\nQueue this work on a dedicated unbound workqueue instead, spanning\nthe CPUs of the CPT table as ptlrpcd threads do, so that the\nscheduler runs it on an idle CPU. This work is needed to free\nmemory, as osc_lru_reserve() waits for it, so give the workqueue\na rescuer thread with WQ_MEM_RECLAIM, which system_wq lacks.\n\nFixes: 776e163cf5 (\"LU-17180 ptlrpc: don't block ptlrpcd too long\")\nSigned-off-by: Sebastien Buisson <sbuisson@ddn.com>\nAssisted-by: Claude:claude-opus-5-5\nChange-Id: I1a409d4f0ae4c77e3169431a1839b83788bcbffc\n"
      },
      "branch": "refs/heads/master"
    }
  },
